 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery** (`docs/decisions/change-delivery-transport.md`). Committed revisions are pushed to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets, backed by replayable revisions in the change store and browser reconnect/catch-up logic. Five-second HTTPS polling (simpler stateless operation, but higher read load and multi-second lag) and a hosted realtime service (lower connection-ops burden, but recurring vendor fees and provider coupling) were rejected: self-hosted WebSockets deliver fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor, accepting connection-capacity and reconnect operations in exchange.
